    "q-48000": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48000",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)+3x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)$ and $h(x)=3x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-3x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=4-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{u} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=3x:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=3\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4-3x}+3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
